00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:33.690
We laid the first copper cable

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:36.660
across the Atlantic Ocean in 1858.

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:38.850
It connected Ireland to Canada.

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:40.590
They sent the first
telegram across this wire

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:42.570
and it took 18 hours to arrive,

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:44.400
which was like light-speed back then.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.250
The cable broke three weeks
later, but even still,

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:49.170
it was a huge success,

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:52.440
a revolution for a world
whose progress hinged on

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:54.004
being more and more connected.

00:04:54.004 --> 00:04:54.893
- [Narrator] Through a single cable,

00:04:54.893 --> 00:04:57.300
only an inch and a quarter thick,

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:58.980
which lies on the ocean bed.

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:00.270
- [Johnny] The planet would be linked up

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:02.040
by many more of these cables

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:03.960
draped along the ocean floor,

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:06.480
allowing us to call
people across the world

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.300
and eventually allowing
us to access information

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:13.020
through webpages hosted
on servers far, far away.

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:15.690
- An online network called internet.

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:20.220
- Today, there are 1.4 million
kilometers worth of cables

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:23.970
linking us all up and they
transfer a ton of data,

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:25.560
like the fastest cables can transfer

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:27.900
224 terabytes per second,

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:30.360
and the amount of data being
sent around the world doubles

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:31.953
every two to three years.

00:05:33.778 --> 00:05:36.150
Unsurprisingly, there's a whole industry

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:38.940
dedicated to just laying down these cables

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:40.890
and then monitoring
them and repairing them

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:42.030
when they break.

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:45.000
Sometimes they get broken by
fishing trawlers or anchors

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.100
or natural disasters.

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:49.230
Sometimes they even get broken by sharks,

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:50.340
though that's pretty rare,

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:54.660
but sometimes these cables
are broken on purpose.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:56.910
There's this island right
off the coast of China,

00:05:56.910 --> 00:05:58.890
but it technically belongs to Taiwan,

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:02.040
the country that China has
vowed to eventually absorb

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:02.873
into their own.

00:06:02.873 --> 00:06:05.070
Needless to say, there's
a lot of tension here

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:06.660
and in the last five years,

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:08.250
the cables of this little island

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:12.360
have accidentally been broken 27 times,

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:14.130
which is a lot for cables.

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:18.240
China denies that they had any
part in these 27 accidents.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:20.250
They say it's just an unfortunate fishing

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:23.550
or anchor incident, but I'm suspicious.

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:26.730
- [Reporter 3] Officials fear
that Matsu is just a warning

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:29.520
and that internet connections
to the whole of Taiwan

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:30.810
could be under threat.

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:33.420
- Sometimes it's unclear
who attacks these cables.

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:36.090
Like a few years ago
when somebody ripped out

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:38.940
a two and a half mile piece of this cable

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:41.520
that connects Norway to
the island of Svalbard.

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:43.920
Other times it's very clear
who's cutting the cables.

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:46.470
Like when the United States
was at war with Saddam Hussein

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:48.870
in the nineties and
they cut Saddam's cable

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:50.583
so that he couldn't communicate.

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:55.230
I mean, cutting the communication
cables of your enemy

00:06:55.230 --> 00:06:56.220
is an old strategy.

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:59.160
They were doing that way back
in the American Civil War,

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:03.330
but the more interesting approach
to me isn't just sabotage.

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:05.850
There's another way
that you can use cables

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:08.820
to your advantage if you are a great power

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:10.383
trying to control things.

00:07:10.383 --> 00:07:12.630
(electronic music)

00:07:12.630 --> 00:07:14.550
If you have access to submarine cables,

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:16.890
which governments and militaries do,

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:20.040
you can scrape through the
insulation of this cable.

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:22.050
You can splice in another cable

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:24.000
and you can duplicate the signal

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.078
and hear everything your enemy is saying.

00:07:26.078 --> 00:07:30.578
(people speaking in foreign language)

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:34.560
The British Navy did this
to a German submarine cable

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:37.020
out here in the middle of the
Atlantic, during World War I.

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:39.300
The Germans suspected that
they were being listened to,

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:42.660
so they sent all their
communication as complex codes,

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:45.060
but the British had
very smart code breakers

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:48.000
and they were able to decrypt all of this

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.860
and discover a lot of Germany's secrets,

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:52.530
and they discovered this
secret plan that Germany had

00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:56.010
to ally with Mexico and
invade the United States.

00:07:56.010 --> 00:07:58.800
This is one reason why the
U.S. ended up joining the war,

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:01.855
and it all happened because
of submarine cable tapping.

00:08:01.855 --> 00:08:03.570
(uptempo beat music)

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:06.720
World War II took cable
tapping to a whole new level.

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:09.330
A more connected world
meant more opportunities

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:12.060
for vital military
information to leak out.

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:14.100
So the U.S. created a new agency

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:15.840
where they would tap in and monitor

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:19.770
hundreds of thousands of civilian
telegrams and phone calls

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:21.060
flowing through both the mail,

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:23.220
but also through these submarine cables.

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:25.440
I mean, think about it,
it was a scary time.

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:27.810
It was global war, it
was mass destruction,

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:31.620
so mass surveillance on
your people felt necessary

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:33.960
in the name of national security.

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:36.360
- [Reporter 4] Even the insides
of envelopes are scrutinized

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:38.280
for hidden writing by these sensors.

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:41.430
- This agency was shut
down after World War II,

00:08:41.430 --> 00:08:44.912
but the seeds of mass
surveillance were now planted.

00:08:44.912 --> 00:08:47.040
(techno music)

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:48.060
A few years later,

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:50.910
President Harry Truman creates
a new secret department,

00:08:50.910 --> 00:08:53.580
the National Security Agency or NSA.

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:57.390
Their job was to secretly
collect and analyze communication

00:08:57.390 --> 00:08:58.920
happening in the country,

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:00.690
communication that was not meant for them,

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:03.600
spying on communication of all kinds,

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:05.853
all in the name of national security.

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:10.650
By the time the United
States and the Soviet Union

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:15.120
are locked in a Cold War, spying
becomes even more valuable,

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:17.430
even more of a priority
for these governments,

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:20.613
and once again, underwater
cables become a target.

00:09:23.310 --> 00:09:26.130
The Americans built this
entire spy submarine,

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:29.880
which had a secret space set
aside for intelligence officers

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:31.230
and a giant computer.

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:33.630
They called this space the bat cave.

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:36.510
Of course, they did, a
bunch of military dudes

00:09:36.510 --> 00:09:39.030
on a submarine and they've
got their secret computers,

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:40.710
like, "We're in the bat cave."

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:41.970
I can totally see it.

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:44.220
This submarine called the USS Halibut

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:46.890
had a little mini-submarine-looking thing

00:09:46.890 --> 00:09:48.930
that was actually not a mini-submarine,

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:52.290
but rather a pressurized
chamber full of special gas

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:55.110
that divers would just
sit in there and breathe.

00:09:55.110 --> 00:09:58.050
It was pressurized to
feel like the deep ocean

00:09:58.050 --> 00:10:00.229
so that these divers could
go out into the ocean

00:10:00.229 --> 00:10:03.210
and be 400 feet under the surface

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:05.160
and their bodies were
ready for the pressure.

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:09.240
They used this to secretly
navigate to underwater cables.

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:11.970
The one we know about happened
over here right off the coast

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:12.960
of Eastern Russia.

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:15.330
The divers would leave their
pressurized gas chamber.

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:17.370
They would navigate this big, clunky

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:20.130
electric listening device onto the cable.

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:22.650
It's basically a giant 20' recorder

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:24.631
that would tap into the cable and record

00:10:24.631 --> 00:10:26.190
everything passing through.

00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:29.460
They somehow parsed through the
dozens of different signals,

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:32.340
specific phone calls
between Soviet officials,

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:33.930
but of course, this is the analog days,

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:36.390
so the storage on this
device would fill up,

00:10:36.390 --> 00:10:39.180
so they had to go back with
their little bat cave submarine

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:42.000
every few months to replace the tape.

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:44.280
They did this for 10 years.

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:46.080
This was called Operation Ivy Bells,

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:47.880
and we talked about it a little
bit in the whole deep dive

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:49.350
we did on submarines.

00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:52.050
But boy, it just never
ceases to blow my mind

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:54.783
how resource-intensive this operation was.

00:10:55.697 --> 00:10:57.300
(water bubbles)

00:10:57.300 --> 00:10:59.880
Anyway, through this
tapping on the Soviet cable,

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:03.030
the Americans learned just
how scared the Soviets were

00:11:03.030 --> 00:11:05.160
by the buildup of nuclear weapons.

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:06.990
This helped them negotiate a slowdown

00:11:06.990 --> 00:11:09.870
in the nuclear arms race
and even helped lead to

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:12.453
the end of the Cold War
according to one expert.

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:15.990
By the early nineties,
the Cold War was over.

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:18.000
- [President Clinton]
The change of centuries,

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:20.010
the dawning of a new millennium.

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:22.020
- But then came a world changing attack

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:24.660
into the ultraconnected internet age.

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:25.950
- This new law that I signed today

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:28.470
will allow surveillance
of all communications

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:31.020
used by terrorists, including emails,

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:32.790
the internet, and cell phones.

00:11:32.790 --> 00:11:34.963
This government will enforce this law

00:11:34.963 --> 00:11:39.300
with all the urgency of a nation at war.

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:40.980
It is now my honor to sign into law

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:43.890
the USA Patriot Act of 2001.

00:11:44.988 --> 00:11:47.988
(audience applauds)

00:11:49.752 --> 00:11:53.040
- In 2006, a leaked document revealed that

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:55.650
in a nondescript building in San Francisco

00:11:55.650 --> 00:11:59.640
behind this random
yellow door labeled 641A,

00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:02.460
the NSA had set up this
harmless-looking box

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:05.070
that tapped into the fiber optic cables

00:12:05.070 --> 00:12:07.650
that traversed the
Pacific Ocean into Asia.

00:12:07.650 --> 00:12:11.280
The phone company AT&amp;T had
agreed to let them siphon off

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:14.400
the traffic that was moving
through their cables.

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:17.010
This seemed kind of nuts that
the U.S. was tapping a cable

00:12:17.010 --> 00:12:19.380
where all of our
communication goes through,

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:21.390
but we had no idea what was coming.

00:12:21.390 --> 00:12:24.240
In 2013, Edward Snowden,

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:27.600
an NSA contractor unleashed
one of the biggest leaks

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:28.710
in American history.

00:12:28.710 --> 00:12:30.120
- This is the greatest hemorrhaging

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:31.590
of a legitimate American secrets

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:33.000
in the history of the Republic.

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:35.400
- And that is what we
have been looking through.

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:36.750
I mean, there's a lot more documents,

00:12:36.750 --> 00:12:39.510
but we sifted through
and found the documents

00:12:39.510 --> 00:12:42.180
that show us that it wasn't just one cable

00:12:42.180 --> 00:12:43.860
in San Francisco being tapped.

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:47.460
The NSA was targeting every single cable

00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:49.770
that touched the United States,

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:52.590
a huge amount of global traffic, email,

00:12:52.590 --> 00:12:54.270
text messages, phone calls.

00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:55.380
They had cooperation from

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:58.080
all these telecommunication
and tech companies to do this.

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:02.160
Microsoft, Yahoo, Google,
Facebook, PalTalk?

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:03.450
The hell is PalTalk?

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:05.850
Who knows, but the NSA
was tapping their cables.

00:13:06.690 --> 00:13:10.530
YouTube, Skype, AOL,
and even Apple by 2012,

00:13:10.530 --> 00:13:12.300
and their goal in all of this,

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:15.120
well, according to one of these slides,

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:20.120
the goal was to sniff it all,
know it all, collect it all,

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:24.510
process it all, exploit
it all, partner it all.

00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:26.700
In other words, according
to another leaked slide,

00:13:26.700 --> 00:13:29.130
to master the internet.

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:30.450
There's this one spreadsheet

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:32.610
in all of these leaked
documents that really said it,

00:13:32.610 --> 00:13:33.600
here it is.

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:37.440
This is a list, a spreadsheet
of some of the cables

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:39.900
that the NSA had access to,

00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:41.340
we literally know which ones.

00:13:41.340 --> 00:13:44.130
I mean, we have the data
of all of these cables.

00:13:44.130 --> 00:13:45.780
We could literally map all of these

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:47.880
from the southern cross cable

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:50.040
that connects California to Australia,

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:53.730
to the Apollo cable that
connects New York to France,

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:56.160
to the tiny cables that
connect Puerto Rico

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:57.960
to the British Virgin Islands.

00:13:57.960 --> 00:13:59.894
All in all, by 2009,

00:13:59.894 --> 00:14:04.170
the NSA had stuck a probe in
hundreds of submarine cables

00:14:04.170 --> 00:14:05.490
all around the globe.

00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:08.370
Oh, and they didn't do it alone.

00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:09.870
The NSA partnered with

00:14:09.870 --> 00:14:13.530
the OG masters of
Cable-tapping, the British.

00:14:13.530 --> 00:14:17.760
- Since the 1940s, GCHQ and
its American equivalence

00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:20.610
have had a relationship
that is unique in the world.

00:14:20.610 --> 00:14:22.680
- Okay, so there's a
weird loophole thing here.

00:14:22.680 --> 00:14:26.070
U.S. law allows the NSA to track anyone

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:27.720
outside of the United States,

00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:29.190
but there are restrictions

00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:31.710
when it comes to tracking
American citizens.

00:14:31.710 --> 00:14:33.120
So to get around that,

00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:36.480
they turn to their British
counterparts, the GCHQ.

00:14:36.480 --> 00:14:39.060
So over the course of a couple
years in the early 2010s,

00:14:39.060 --> 00:14:43.530
the NSA is paying 100 million
pounds in secret money

00:14:43.530 --> 00:14:45.660
to gain access to all of this data,

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:49.080
this data that they call
a gold mine to exploit,

00:14:49.080 --> 00:14:53.500
hundreds of thousands of names,
over 76,000 geocoordinates,

00:14:53.500 --> 00:14:58.200
194 million messages collected per day.

00:14:58.200 --> 00:14:59.910
And unlike the NSA,

00:14:59.910 --> 00:15:02.880
the British could actually
spy on American targets,

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:06.150
but the point is they were
in it to collect it all.

00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:09.240
- If information arrives in
the UK from the United States,

00:15:09.240 --> 00:15:12.000
it's governed by our laws.

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:14.628
The system works as intended.

00:15:14.628 --> 00:15:17.140
(suspenseful music)

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:19.740
- Okay, okay, yeah, it's a lot of data,

00:15:19.740 --> 00:15:24.000
but what can you do with
600 million phone events

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:25.500
worth of data?

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:28.500
That's way too much for any
human to actually process,

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:31.320
and this is where it
gets kind of nuts to me.

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:33.930
We've got this slide, which by the way,

00:15:33.930 --> 00:15:37.080
I know that we're dealing with
really top secret documents,

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:41.430
but they all have clip-art
and these templatized

00:15:41.430 --> 00:15:44.657
Windows 95-looking
titles, freaking love it.

00:15:44.657 --> 00:15:46.470
(jazzy drum music)

00:15:46.470 --> 00:15:50.550
This slide, this complicated,
wonky, wonky slide

00:15:50.550 --> 00:15:53.280
with this flow chart is
the answer to what they do

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:56.253
with all of this data, but
here's basically how it works.

00:15:57.180 --> 00:15:59.400
So the NSA gets all of this data

00:15:59.400 --> 00:16:00.720
of all of our communication,

00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:03.330
either from cables or
from phone companies.

00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:04.470
They funnel it through

00:16:04.470 --> 00:16:07.713
this central processing
facility called Pinecone.

00:16:11.550 --> 00:16:13.890
This is presumably
where they're processing

00:16:13.890 --> 00:16:17.100
a lot of this data and
trying to find patterns.

00:16:17.100 --> 00:16:20.970
The NSA says that their job is
to find suspected terrorists

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:23.640
or other people who want to
do harm to national security.

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:26.520
And to do this, they use
computers and algorithms

00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:29.490
to make links between suspected terrorists

00:16:29.490 --> 00:16:32.280
and their immediate network,
the people they call,

00:16:32.280 --> 00:16:34.560
the people they talk to, where they are.

00:16:34.560 --> 00:16:37.020
They say they only hold
on to those records

00:16:37.020 --> 00:16:38.790
and they throw out everything else.

00:16:38.790 --> 00:16:41.340
We don't know if that's true,
but let's just assume it is.

00:16:41.340 --> 00:16:42.936
And then once it's all
processed and sorted,

00:16:42.936 --> 00:16:47.936
it goes into a little searchable
database called XKeyscore,

00:16:48.150 --> 00:16:49.860
which is a very clunky name,

00:16:49.860 --> 00:16:51.960
but hey, great graphic design once again.

00:16:51.960 --> 00:16:54.750
This is basically like
a internal search engine

00:16:54.750 --> 00:16:57.060
that catalogs all of the metadata

00:16:57.060 --> 00:16:58.740
that has been assigned to

00:16:58.740 --> 00:17:02.640
all of this call message
geolocation information

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:04.020
and makes it searchable.

00:17:04.020 --> 00:17:06.150
So you could pull up a
person, a suspected terrorist,

00:17:06.150 --> 00:17:08.730
and you could find everything
you need to know about

00:17:08.730 --> 00:17:12.450
who they talk to, where they
are, what their emails say,

00:17:12.450 --> 00:17:15.810
or in their words, anything
you wish to extract.

00:17:15.810 --> 00:17:19.350
It's all searchable on
this convenient platform.

00:17:19.350 --> 00:17:21.390
This allows them to do searches like

00:17:21.390 --> 00:17:23.850
let's look for an Arabic font,

00:17:23.850 --> 00:17:27.630
Google query coming out of
the tribal areas of Pakistan,

00:17:27.630 --> 00:17:29.550
and boom, they're linked in.

00:17:29.550 --> 00:17:33.180
It's all right here in
XKeyscore the database.

00:17:33.180 --> 00:17:35.337
Because of the globally connected world,

00:17:35.337 --> 00:17:38.850
the NSA now has access to this person.

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:40.920
They can see what
messages they're sending.

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:43.080
They can see where they are right now,

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:44.670
assuming they have their phone,

00:17:44.670 --> 00:17:46.890
all searchable in this
convenient database,

00:17:46.890 --> 00:17:49.410
accessible to agencies
from other countries

00:17:49.410 --> 00:17:50.850
and all over the world.

00:17:50.850 --> 00:17:53.460
The whole assumption here is that in order

00:17:53.460 --> 00:17:57.090
to find your target, the
terrorist, the spy, whatever,

00:17:57.090 --> 00:18:00.060
you need to access all of the information,

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:03.810
this turns metadata into a
weapon of modern warfare.

00:18:03.810 --> 00:18:05.943
- We kill people based on metadata,

00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:11.301
but that's not what we
do with this metadata.

00:18:11.301 --> 00:18:12.180
(suspenseful music)

00:18:12.180 --> 00:18:14.250
- This revelation, all of these documents,

00:18:14.250 --> 00:18:17.850
this whole Snowden drama was a big deal

00:18:17.850 --> 00:18:19.080
for a lot of reasons.

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:20.280
One of the things it did

00:18:20.280 --> 00:18:24.660
is it made the location of
cables really important.

00:18:24.660 --> 00:18:27.390
Most of the cables were going
through the United States,

00:18:27.390 --> 00:18:28.890
but after this leak,

00:18:28.890 --> 00:18:31.230
countries started to look
for alternative routes

00:18:31.230 --> 00:18:33.360
that bypassed the United States,

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:35.820
like this one that connects
Brazil to South Africa

00:18:35.820 --> 00:18:37.530
and then goes on to Asia.

00:18:37.530 --> 00:18:39.510
So over the last decade since these leaks,

00:18:39.510 --> 00:18:42.030
the U.S. has become less
and less the epicenter

00:18:42.030 --> 00:18:45.240
of cable connection, and you guessed it,

00:18:45.240 --> 00:18:46.680
there's another player in town

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:49.761
who wants access to cables, China.

00:18:49.761 --> 00:18:51.510
(suspenseful music)

00:18:51.510 --> 00:18:52.980
China is rapidly building out

00:18:52.980 --> 00:18:54.930
their worldwide network of cables,

00:18:54.930 --> 00:18:55.763
and they're doing this through

00:18:55.763 --> 00:18:58.170
Chinese state-owned companies like Huawei.

00:18:58.170 --> 00:19:00.210
And this is adding yet another front

00:19:00.210 --> 00:19:03.630
in this emerging Cold War
tension between great powers

00:19:03.630 --> 00:19:05.550
that want to lead the global system.

00:19:05.550 --> 00:19:09.270
It turns out that controlling
and spying on cables

00:19:09.270 --> 00:19:13.440
is a requirement for global
leadership in 2023, who knew?

00:19:13.440 --> 00:19:17.010
But unlike the U.S. who
purports to protect privacy

00:19:17.010 --> 00:19:19.770
and civil liberties, but
secretly violates them,

00:19:19.770 --> 00:19:21.420
China doesn't even pretend.

00:19:21.420 --> 00:19:24.060
China has built a society
off of mass surveillance

00:19:24.060 --> 00:19:26.280
and control of information.

00:19:26.280 --> 00:19:29.400
Like here's actual footage of
how China tracks the movement

00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:32.340
of its population through
visual recognition,

00:19:32.340 --> 00:19:35.400
through cataloging every face, every car.

00:19:35.400 --> 00:19:38.670
Information is power in this day and age,

00:19:38.670 --> 00:19:39.600
and just a couple years ago,

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:42.600
the U.S. formed a new special
unit with intelligence

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:44.340
and regulation officials.

00:19:44.340 --> 00:19:46.290
It's a group called Team Telecom,

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:49.720
and their job is to assess any
new projects, any new cables,

00:19:49.720 --> 00:19:52.920
and make sure that they aren't going to

00:19:52.920 --> 00:19:55.470
threaten or weaken U.S. interests.

00:19:55.470 --> 00:19:56.820
For example, there was this one cable

00:19:56.820 --> 00:19:59.400
that was supposed to
connect LA to Hong Kong.

00:19:59.400 --> 00:20:01.920
It was being built by Google and Facebook,

00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:03.420
and they were into the project,

00:20:03.420 --> 00:20:05.850
hundreds of millions of
dollars into the project,

00:20:05.850 --> 00:20:09.780
and this Team Telecoms
shut it down, too risky.

00:20:09.780 --> 00:20:11.482
We can't have our fiber optic cables

00:20:11.482 --> 00:20:15.030
going too close to China
less they spy on us.

00:20:15.030 --> 00:20:17.550
This is making the map look really weird.

00:20:17.550 --> 00:20:18.960
You used to only have to have

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:21.390
one major set of cables
connecting countries,

00:20:21.390 --> 00:20:24.000
but now you're starting to see duplicates.

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:26.310
The Chinese lines and the American lines,

00:20:26.310 --> 00:20:28.320
each building out their own network,

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:30.270
not connecting to each other.

00:20:30.270 --> 00:20:32.460
It's like the perfect
symbol of the tension

00:20:32.460 --> 00:20:34.980
that is rising between
these two superpowers.

00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:37.470
The bifurcated world
that we're moving into.

00:20:37.470 --> 00:20:40.530
China is connecting these
small remote islands

00:20:40.530 --> 00:20:41.940
laying down cables.

00:20:41.940 --> 00:20:45.000
And these islanders are
happy to have fast internet,

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:48.300
even if it comes from China
and is probably being tapped.

00:20:48.300 --> 00:20:49.440
The continent of Africa,

00:20:49.440 --> 00:20:53.010
the U.S. and China are both
laying down competing lines.

00:20:53.010 --> 00:20:54.330
The country's caught in the middle of this

00:20:54.330 --> 00:20:55.440
understand what's going on.

00:20:55.440 --> 00:20:58.290
Two, aspiring hegemons vying for power,

00:20:58.290 --> 00:21:01.110
each involved in spying
and sucking up information

00:21:01.110 --> 00:21:02.280
in their own way,

00:21:02.280 --> 00:21:05.250
but I guess it's worth it,
because it means faster internet,

00:21:05.250 --> 00:21:06.690
it means connection to the world.

00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:08.550
Russia is even trying to get in on this

00:21:08.550 --> 00:21:12.210
with their aptly named
Polar Express cable.

00:21:12.210 --> 00:21:14.870
And even though the old
school tapping into cables

00:21:14.870 --> 00:21:16.860
in the middle of the ocean
to spy on your neighbor

00:21:16.860 --> 00:21:18.450
is kind of outdated now,

00:21:18.450 --> 00:21:21.300
there's some evidence that
Russia might be still doing this.

00:21:21.300 --> 00:21:23.370
They've got this one
ship called the Yantar.

00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:25.230
It's a surface ship that
is generally understood

00:21:25.230 --> 00:21:26.400
to be a spy vessel,

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:28.710
and it's been spotted on the surface above

00:21:28.710 --> 00:21:31.440
where we know submarine
cables are near Ireland

00:21:31.440 --> 00:21:32.400
or near Syria.

00:21:32.400 --> 00:21:34.200
Some think that they're
just tapping into them

00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:35.190
like the old days,

00:21:35.190 --> 00:21:37.650
maybe using one of these mini-submarines.

00:21:37.650 --> 00:21:39.180
But even if they're not tapping into them,

00:21:39.180 --> 00:21:42.810
they're very presence above
the cable is kind of a threat.

00:21:42.810 --> 00:21:45.000
They could cut this cable at any moment.

00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:46.920
The U.S. who is very good at submarines

00:21:46.920 --> 00:21:48.780
has its own spy vessels,

00:21:48.780 --> 00:21:51.900
the most secretive one
being the USS Jimmy Carter,

00:21:51.900 --> 00:21:54.030
and we have no idea what it's up to.

00:21:54.030 --> 00:21:55.203
Security experts speculate

00:21:55.203 --> 00:21:58.620
that it's probably tapping
into cables for some reason,

00:21:58.620 --> 00:22:00.450
somewhere at the bottom of the ocean.

00:22:00.450 --> 00:22:02.467
But a lot of people in
the industry are like,

00:22:02.467 --> 00:22:05.910
"No, that's so old days, why bother?"

00:22:05.910 --> 00:22:09.150
It's so much easier to
just tap into them on land.

00:22:09.150 --> 00:22:10.770
You get way more data
and you don't have to

00:22:10.770 --> 00:22:12.000
go to the bottom of the ocean.

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:14.640
Meanwhile, the private companies
in charge of these cables

00:22:14.640 --> 00:22:16.950
have started to up their own defenses.

00:22:16.950 --> 00:22:18.510
They're taking all
these extra precautions,

00:22:18.510 --> 00:22:22.260
including sending out
these submarine drones

00:22:22.260 --> 00:22:23.640
that just spend their time

00:22:23.640 --> 00:22:26.010
tracing along the bottom of the ocean,

00:22:26.010 --> 00:22:28.200
babysitting these cables and making sure

00:22:28.200 --> 00:22:30.453
that there's no damage
or tampering going on.

00:22:31.980 --> 00:22:34.110
So I feel like I'm
gonna say what I've said

00:22:34.110 --> 00:22:35.790
a bunch on the channel lately,

00:22:35.790 --> 00:22:40.440
which is we are entering a
new chapter in geopolitics.

00:22:40.440 --> 00:22:42.720
A globalized world is hyperconnected,

00:22:42.720 --> 00:22:44.760
but suddenly and unexpectedly,

00:22:44.760 --> 00:22:47.550
we're seeing a new set of divisions,

00:22:47.550 --> 00:22:49.860
countries not trusting each other anymore.

00:22:49.860 --> 00:22:53.100
Nations wanting to create
their own separate systems,

00:22:53.100 --> 00:22:55.290
both economically, militarily,

00:22:55.290 --> 00:22:58.140
but also with the
infrastructure of the internet.

00:22:58.140 --> 00:23:01.920
A chapter where countries now
are more skeptical than ever,

00:23:01.920 --> 00:23:03.630
especially great powers,

00:23:03.630 --> 00:23:06.660
great powers that are building
their own infrastructure

00:23:06.660 --> 00:23:09.810
so that they can communicate
and control information,

00:23:09.810 --> 00:23:12.030
not trusting the other half of the world

00:23:12.030 --> 00:23:13.620
to handle their data.

00:23:13.620 --> 00:23:16.350
We see Navies with secret
ships that cost close to

00:23:16.350 --> 00:23:18.060
a $1 billion a piece,

00:23:18.060 --> 00:23:21.210
and then we see everyone
else caught in the middle.

00:23:21.210 --> 00:23:25.163
And in the end, most of
us just want fast internet

00:23:25.163 --> 00:23:27.219
connection with friends around the world

00:23:27.219 --> 00:23:30.552
and a feeling that we're safe.

00:23:30.552 --> 00:23:33.552
(suspenseful music)

00:23:48.420 --> 00:23:51.048
All right, thanks for
watching today's video.

00:23:51.048 --> 00:23:52.710
Just a little nugget here.

00:23:52.710 --> 00:23:54.900
We did this video,

00:23:54.900 --> 00:23:58.230
because we did the deep dive on submarines

00:23:58.230 --> 00:23:59.063
a few months ago.

00:23:59.063 --> 00:23:59.910
I don't know if anyone saw that,

00:23:59.910 --> 00:24:03.060
but it was in that reporting
that we came across

00:24:03.060 --> 00:24:05.280
the spying element of all of this.

00:24:05.280 --> 00:24:07.290
And I remember I asked all of you,

00:24:07.290 --> 00:24:08.850
do you want me to do this video?

00:24:08.850 --> 00:24:10.020
And you all said, yes.

00:24:10.020 --> 00:24:12.570
I got like so many comments
that were like, "Yes."

00:24:12.570 --> 00:24:14.100
And so, we did it.

00:24:14.100 --> 00:24:17.220
And that is one thing I love about YouTube

00:24:17.220 --> 00:24:19.170
and being an independent
journalist on YouTube

00:24:19.170 --> 00:24:21.540
is that I get to just ask you,

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:22.477
I'm talking to you and be like,

00:24:22.477 --> 00:24:23.550
"Hey, do you want this?"

00:24:23.550 --> 00:24:24.480
And you're like, "Yes."

00:24:24.480 --> 00:24:26.310
And I go, "Okay," and I make it.

00:24:26.310 --> 00:24:29.310
And there's no corporate
middleman who decides

00:24:29.310 --> 00:24:30.480
what we make.

00:24:30.480 --> 00:24:31.740
We make what you guys wanna see.

00:24:31.740 --> 00:24:35.730
So as always, you can pitch us ideas

00:24:35.730 --> 00:24:38.160
and we will hear them.

00:24:38.160 --> 00:24:41.070
For those who want to support
more fully in our kind of

00:24:41.070 --> 00:24:42.210
more intimate community,

00:24:42.210 --> 00:24:44.520
we have a Patreon called the Newsroom.

00:24:44.520 --> 00:24:47.010
The Newsroom is a place
where you can support

00:24:47.010 --> 00:24:49.410
what we do here, but you also
get in on a few benefits,

00:24:49.410 --> 00:24:52.710
including an extra behind
the scenes video every month

00:24:52.710 --> 00:24:55.860
where you get to see all of
the people and the processes

00:24:55.860 --> 00:24:59.370
and the shenanigans that
happen here in the studio.

00:24:59.370 --> 00:25:01.050
We have a big team and
you get to meet them,

00:25:01.050 --> 00:25:02.160
if you are a member of the newsroom.

00:25:02.160 --> 00:25:03.690
You get access to my scripts,

00:25:03.690 --> 00:25:05.820
you get access to Tom Fox music.

00:25:05.820 --> 00:25:08.280
But really you get access
to the warm fuzzy feeling

00:25:08.280 --> 00:25:10.230
that you're supporting a YouTube channel

00:25:10.230 --> 00:25:12.570
that is trying to make rigorous journalism

00:25:12.570 --> 00:25:16.470
in a time where facts and
truth are kind of eroding.

00:25:16.470 --> 00:25:19.380
So if that floats your boat,
we would love your support.

00:25:19.380 --> 00:25:21.270
We also have luts and presets,

00:25:21.270 --> 00:25:24.600
which are like the thing
we use to color our videos

00:25:24.600 --> 00:25:25.470
and our photos.

00:25:25.470 --> 00:25:27.570
You could buy those and they
help support the channel.

00:25:27.570 --> 00:25:29.490
We have a poster that I designed.

00:25:29.490 --> 00:25:31.710
I'm super into map projections,

00:25:31.710 --> 00:25:33.870
all the different ways
you can show a map shape,

00:25:33.870 --> 00:25:37.200
and I made a beautiful poster that has it.

00:25:37.200 --> 00:25:38.400
If you have that poster,

00:25:38.400 --> 00:25:39.900
I think a lot of you have
purchased that poster,

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:42.810
we actually sold out and
we're reprinting a new batch,

00:25:42.810 --> 00:25:44.430
so they should be in there.

00:25:44.430 --> 00:25:46.200
Don't quote me on that because
they could be sold out again.

00:25:46.200 --> 00:25:49.620
But anyway, if you have that
poster, tag me or something,

00:25:49.620 --> 00:25:51.960
I wanna see what it looks
like out in the wild.

00:25:51.960 --> 00:25:53.430
I looked at it on a screen for months

00:25:53.430 --> 00:25:54.480
and now I have some here,

00:25:54.480 --> 00:25:56.460
but I don't know what it
looks like out in the wild,

00:25:56.460 --> 00:25:57.960
so share it with me.

00:25:57.960 --> 00:25:58.793
I think that's it.

00:25:58.793 --> 00:26:00.140
Anyway, that's all I've got.

00:26:00.140 --> 00:26:01.950
Thank you all for being here,

00:26:01.950 --> 00:26:05.823
and I'll see you in the
next video, bye-bye.

00:26:07.680 --> 00:26:09.720
- [Narrator] Although the
calls through the cable

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:13.110
will be from many
nations, in many tongues,

00:26:13.110 --> 00:26:16.500
the cable itself speaks
a single language to all

00:26:16.500 --> 00:26:20.250
that is the language of
friendship and cooperation

00:26:20.250 --> 00:26:24.240
between the men and women of
France, West Germany, England,

00:26:24.240 --> 00:26:25.740
and the United States

00:26:25.740 --> 00:26:28.650
who conceived and brought to completion

00:26:28.650 --> 00:26:30.453
the cable to the continent.

